body.en:
Solving a quadratic equation by the factor method is always these three moves:
(1) expand and move every term to one side so that the right-hand side is 0;
(2) factor out the common factor or use the cross-method;
(3) set each factor to 0 and write down ALL the roots.
Example: {{math:0}}
The classic loss of marks: when both sides contain $x$ (or the same bracket), cancelling it assumes it is not 0, so a root disappears:
✗ Wrong (cancelling $x$): {{math:1}} → only $x=-8$ is left, $x=0$ is gone
✓ Right (expand, move to one side, factor out): {{math:2}}

