body.en:
A Paper 2 favourite: given one root, find the value of another expression. Two steps only:
(1) write down $a\alpha^{2}+b\alpha+c=0$;
(2) build the target expression as a multiple of $\alpha^{2}$ (or $a\alpha^{2}$) and substitute.
Example: {{math:0}}
There is no need to find the value of $\alpha$ itself (it is usually irrational) — the root relation is enough.
